      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 2 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5 (water meters, R49). The fragment contains:\n\n- Page 4 of 11: Table 3 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\n- Page 5 of 11: continuation of reduced bore table (DN500, DN600), then Table 4 General characteristics — Full bore - AC mains powered\n- Page 6 of 11: continuation of full bore table\n\nCertificate-level info:\n- number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\n- revision: 5\n- project_number: 2496280\n- page_total: 11\n- member_state: The Netherlands\n- scheme: A (from \"2013-A-NL1\")\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1 (possibly)\n\nThe instruction says: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nSo I should include certificate info since it's present (number, project_number, page_total, member_state), and the matrix tables.\n\nThis is a family of water meters (R49) with models varying by meter size (DN40 ... DN1650) and configuration axes: reduced bore (battery/renewable energy powered) vs full bore (AC mains powered), and accuracy class 1 vs 2.\n\nStructure:\n\nThe tables have columns: Meter size, Accuracy class, Minimum Q1, Transitional Q2, Permanent Q3, Overload Q4, Ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nThere are two configuration groups:\n1. Reduced bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered (Table 3)\n2. Full bore - AC mains powered (Table 4)\n\nWithin each table, some meter sizes have two rows (accuracy class 1 and 2 separately), while others have \"1 & 2\" in one row.\n\nBest approach: \n- matrix_tables: two entries, one for Table 3 (reduced bore) and one for Table 4 (full bore), each with columns meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1.\n- model_family: models = DN sizes? There are many DN sizes. Actually, the axis might be config-level: bore type (reduced/full) × power source. The models are DN sizes.\n\nI think the best structure:\n- model_family with models being each meter size (DN40 ... DN1650) — or each meter size × accuracy class combination.\n\nActually the model_id could be \"DN40\" etc. The accuracy class is an attribute. But accuracy class varies per row too — for reduced bore DN65, class 1 has different Q1/Q2 than class 2. So the matrix rows are (meter size, accuracy class) pairs.\n\nGiven schema constraints, I could:\n- model_family.models: one per DN size (the model identifier is the DN size). \n- characteristics.config_level: attributes varying by configuration axis \"bore_type_power\" (reduced bore battery/renewable vs full bore AC mains) and accuracy_class.\n\nAlternatively, keep it simpler: matrix_tables for both tables (full rows preserved), plus model_level characteristics per (meter size) with values per config.\n\nHmm. The model_level format: {attribute, unit, values: [{model, value}]}. But values also vary by config axis (bore type, accuracy class). The schema allows model_level values with model keys; config_level with {condition, value}.\n\nGiven complexity, I think the cleanest:\n\n1. matrix_tables: two tables (Table 3, Table 4) with all rows.\n2. model_family: models = unique DN sizes.\n3. characteristics.config_level: for attributes q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1, with axis \"meter_size_and_accuracy_class_by_bore_type\" — conditions like \"Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable energy powered, DN40, class 1&2\".\n\nThat would be huge. Alternatively, model_level entries per attribute with model = \"DN40 reduced bore class 1&2\" etc. That's a lot of values but it's accurate.\n\nGiven the instruction says the fragment should be extracted, and the schema has config_level for attribute × config-axis matrix, I'll use config_level with axis describing the dimension.\n\nActually, let me think about what's most useful and compliant. The rules say:\n\n\"**Configuration axes.** When attributes vary by configuration (not by model — e.g. 2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups; static vs dynamic weighing), use `characteristics.config_level` with `axis` describing the dimension. Values follow the same structured form: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`.\"\n\nHere, the meter sizes are arguably models (model family = instruments differing in meter size). The bore type / power source is a configuration axis. Accuracy class is another dimension.\n\nSo structure:\n- model_family.family_name: null (no explicit family name; \"family of instruments\" is mentioned in table titles — could use \"family of instruments\"? Better null).\n- model_family.models: each DN size: DN40, DN50, ..., DN1650. Let me enumerate unique sizes.\n\nReduced bore (Table 3): DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\nFull bore (Table 4): DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650.\n\nUnion: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650. That's 28 models.\n\n- characteristics.config_level: entries for each attribute with axis describing the configuration. The condition strings would encode bore type, power source, meter size, accuracy class. E.g.:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"bore_type_and_power_source × meter_size × accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable energy powered, DN40, accuracy class 1&2\", \"value\": 0.0625, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nThat's 55 rows for table 3 (let me count: DN40, DN50 = 2; DN65 2 rows; DN80 2; DN100 2; DN125 2; DN150 1; DN200 1; DN250 2; DN300 2; DN350 2; DN400 2; DN450 2; then DN500, DN600 = 2 → total: 2+2+2+2+2+1+1+2+2+2+2+2+2 = let me recount from markdown:\n\nTable 3 rows:\n1. DN40, 1&2\n2. DN50, 1&2\n3. DN65, 1\n4. DN65, 2\n5. DN80, 1\n6. DN80, 2\n7. DN100, 1\n8. DN100, 2\n9. DN125, 1\n10. DN125, 2\n11. DN150, 1&2\n12. DN200, 1&2\n13. DN250, 1\n14. DN250, 2\n15. DN300, 1\n16. DN300, 2\n17. DN350, 1\n18. DN350, 2\n19. DN400, 1\n20. DN400, 2\n21. DN450, 1\n22. DN450, 2\n23. DN500, 1 (page 5)\n24. DN500, 2\n25. DN600, 1\n26. DN600, 2\n\nTotal 26 rows for reduced bore.\n\nTable 4 rows (full bore):\n1. DN40, 1&2\n2. DN50, 1&2\n3. DN65, 1&2\n4. DN80, 1&2\n5. DN100, 1&2\n6. DN125, 1&2\n7. DN150, 1\n8. DN150, 2\n9. DN200, 1\n10. DN200, 2\n11. DN250, 1\n12. DN250, 2\n13. DN300, 1\n14. DN300, 2\n15. DN350, 1&2\n16. DN400, 1&2\n17. DN450, 1&2\n18. DN500, 1&2\n19. DN600, 1&2\n20. DN700, 1\n21. DN700, 2\n22. DN750, 1\n23. DN750, 2\n24. DN800, 1\n25. DN800, 2\n26. DN900, 1\n27. DN900, 2\n28. DN1000, 1\n29. DN1000, 2\n30. DN1050, 1\n31. DN1050, 2\n32. DN1100, 1\n33. DN1100, 2\n34. DN1200, 1\n35. DN1200, 2\n36. DN1350, 1\n37. DN1350, 2\n38. DN1400, 1\n39. DN1400, 2\n40. DN1500, 1\n41. DN1500, 2\n42. DN1600, 1\n43. DN1600, 2\n44. DN1650, 1\n\nTotal 44 rows for full bore.\n\nCombined 70 rows × 5 attributes = 350 config values. That's huge but accurate. Alternatively, just use matrix_tables for the raw data and provide config_level in a more compact form? The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. Given these tables ARE multi-dimensional (bore type, power, size, class, 5 flow attributes), matrix_tables is the natural home.\n\nBut rule 4 says for explicit 2-D tables: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So I should populate model_level too.\n\nModel-level: which attribute varies by model (DN size)? q3_permanent (permanent flow Q3) varies by DN size. Also q4, q1, q2, ratio. But they vary by config too.\n\nOption: model_level with attribute \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", values per model where model = \"DN40\" etc., but value differs between reduced/full bore and class. Hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I could make model_id encode size + bore/power, e.g. \"DN40 (Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable)\" and \"DN40 (Full bore, AC mains)\". But then accuracy class still splits some.\n\nAlternative cleaner approach: Use config_level for everything, with the axis being a combination, and conditions encoding all dimensions. And also matrix_tables preserving the tables. And model_family.models listing DN sizes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe simplest compliant approach:\n\n- model_family.models: one entry per (meter size) — model_id \"DN40\" ... since DN size is the model designation for water meters. Add attributes in models? Schema allows attributes{} per model. Could include accuracy classes available per model? Eh.\n\n- characteristics.config_level: axis could be \"meter_size_and_accuracy_class_by_variant\" with entries per attribute. Each values entry: condition = \"Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable energy powered, DN40, class 1 & 2\".\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries preserving tables.\n\nGiven the size, the output will be long. That's fine.\n\nLet me decide on attribute names:\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3\n- overload_flow_rate_q4\n- ratio_q3_q1\n\nUnits: m³/h for Q1–Q4; dimensionless (null) for ratio.\n\nNow convert European decimals: 0,0625 → 0.0625; 0,1 → 0.1; 78,75 → 78.75; 157,5 → 157.5; 100,8 → 100.8; 0,128 → 0.128; 0,2016 → 0.2016; 0,32 → 0.32; 0,512 → 0.512; 0,8 → 0.8; 312,5 → 312.5; 1,26 → 1.26; 2,016 → 2.016; 3,2 → 3.2; 5,12 → 5.12; 6,4 → 6.4; 253,9 → 253.9; 406,2 → 406.2; 204,8 → 204.8; 2,52 → 2.52; 4,032 → 4.032; 1,575 → 1.575; 2,52 → 2.52; 15,625 → 15.625; 12,5 → 12.5; 6,25 → 6.25; 0,1575 → 0.1575; 0,252 → 0.252; 0,63 → 0.63; 0,39375 → 0.39375.\n\nNow decide final structure. I'll do:\n\ncertificate: {\n  number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  revision? — schema has number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision isn't in certificate schema; revision_history is for document revisions. The \"revision 5\" here is the certificate revision. Hmm. I could include revision_history: [{revision: \"5\", changes: null}]? The schema for revision_history: revision (string), date, changes. We don't have date or changes. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Maybe skip revision_history since no details. But revision 5 is important info. I could put it in certificate as... schema doesn't allow extra? The schema lists fields; I could add \"revision\": \"5\"? It says follow schema; adding a field may be tolerated. Hmm, safer to omit or include in number? Number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5\". I might keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add certificate.revision \"5\". The schema doesn't have it, but \"Omit sections that are not present\" — doesn't forbid extra fields. I'll add \"revision\": \"5\" — actually risk of schema validation failure. Let me think: the instruction says produce a JSON object following the layered schema. Extra keys are probably acceptable but not guaranteed. I'll include \"revision\": \"5\" since it's meaningful data. Hmm, alternatively omit. I'll include it — many extractors add it. Actually, to be safe, I could put it into number as printed: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 5\"? That pollutes number. I'll add separate \"revision\": \"5\".\n\nscheme: \"A\" (from \"2013-A-NL1\").\n\nproject_number: \"2496280\" — as string or number? Probably string \"2496280\". I'll use string.\n\npage_total: 11.\n\nmember_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"? The number contains \"NL1\" which is the NMi issuer ID (NMi Certin is NL1... actually NMi is designated by NL1? The certificate numbering R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 — NL1 is the issuing authority ID). I'd include oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Hmm, it's inference. The header says \"NMi\" and Member State \"The Netherlands\". NL1 is plausibly the issuer ID embedded in cert number. I'll include \"NL1\" — reasonably safe inference. Or omit. I'll include.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n\nTable 3:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 3 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore, Battery / Renewable energy powered\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"minimum_q1\", \"transitional_q2\", \"permanent_q3\", \"overload_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [ ... 26 rows ... ],\n  \"description\": \"...\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nTable 4 similarly with 44 rows.\n\nRows values: numbers with decimal points.\n\nTable 3 rows:\nDN40, \"1 & 2\", 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\nDN50, \"1 & 2\", 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\nDN65, \"1\", 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\nDN65, \"2\", 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\nDN80, \"1\", 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\nDN80, \"2\", 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\nDN100, \"1\", 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\nDN100, \"2\", 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\nDN125, \"1\", 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\nDN125, \"2\", 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\nDN150, \"1 & 2\", 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\nDN200, \"1 & 2\", 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\nDN250, \"1\", 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\nDN250, \"2\", 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\nDN300, \"1\", 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN300, \"2\", 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\nDN350, \"1\", 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN350, \"2\", 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\nDN400, \"1\", 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN400, \"2\", 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\nDN450, \"1\", 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN450, \"2\", 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\nDN500, \"1\", 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\nDN500, \"2\", 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN600, \"1\", 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\nDN600, \"2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTable 4 rows:\nDN40, \"1 & 2\", 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\nDN50, \"1 & 2\", 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\nDN65, \"1 & 2\", 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\nDN80, \"1 & 2\", 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\nDN100, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\nDN125, \"1 & 2\", 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\nDN150, \"1\", 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\nDN150, \"2\", 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\nDN200, \"1\", 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\nDN200, \"2\", 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\nDN250, \"1\", 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\nDN250, \"2\", 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\nDN300, \"1\", 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\nDN300, \"2\", 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\nDN350, \"1 & 2\", 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN400, \"1 & 2\", 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\nDN450, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN500, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN600, \"1 & 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\nDN700, \"1\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\nDN700, \"2\", 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\nDN750, \"1\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\nDN750, \"2\", 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\nDN800, \"1\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\nDN800, \"2\", 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\nDN900, \"1\", 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63\nDN900, \"2\", 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125\nDN1000, \"1\", 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\nDN1000, \"2\", 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\nDN1050, \"1\", 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\nDN1050, \"2\", 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\nDN1100, \"1\", 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\nDN1100, \"2\", 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\nDN1200, \"1\", 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\nDN1200, \"2\", 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\nDN1350, \"1\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1350, \"2\", 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1400, \"1\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1400, \"2\", 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1500, \"1\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1500, \"2\", 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1600, \"1\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\nDN1600, \"2\", 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\nDN1650, \"1\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nNow model_family: models = 28 unique DN sizes. Should models include attributes? Could add bore/variants per model. Keep simple: model_id only.\n\ncertified_type: category — this is a water meter → \"Water meter\". The category examples list doesn't include water meter but says \"...\". R49 is water meters. The fragment doesn't explicitly say \"water meter\" though... The certificate number R49 indicates water meters. Since fragment doesn't state category explicitly, but rule 8 says don't fabricate. However category is helpful; R49 = water meters is well-known. I'll include certified_type.category: \"Water meter\" — modest inference from R49. Hmm, risk. The fragment title: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\". The recommendation is R49. I think including \"Water meter\" is justified by the recommendation R49 (OIML R 49 Water meters). But should I include recommendation in this fragment? The fragment doesn't state \"OIML R49-1:2013\" — yes it does! \"Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\". So recommendation: id \"R49\", edition... R49-1:2013 → edition 2013? Schema says edition (int). R49-1:2013 edition is 2013? OIML R49-1 was published 2013. I'll set edition: 2013? Hmm, edition could be the year. I'll use 2013. accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nActually the certificate number itself is R49/2013 → recommendation R49, edition year 2013. I'll include recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, accuracy_classes: [1, 2]}.\n\nNow characteristics: Should I emit model_level and/or config_level in addition to matrix_tables? Rule 4 says for explicit 2-D tables: extract matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level. Here we effectively have 4-D data (bore type/power, size, class, attributes). \n\nPlan: \n- characteristics.config_level: five entries (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio) with axis \"variant_bore_type_power × meter_size × accuracy_class\", values listing 70 conditions each. That's 350 value objects — very long but complete. Alternatively, axis per variant: two config_level entries per attribute? The schema: config_level[]: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. The unit_symbol goes... \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". Hmm, but also each value should be structured with unit separation: {value, unit_symbol}? In the R137 example for model_level, values entries are {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} and attribute-level unit_symbol \"m³/h\" is on the attribute entry. For config_level, I'll put unit_symbol at the attribute entry level and value plain in each values item, following the R137 pattern: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...}]}. For config_level, {\"attribute\": ..., \"axis\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"values\": [{\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ...}]}.\n\nGiven output length constraints, 350 entries is a lot but let me estimate: each value object ~ 100 chars → 35 KB per... hmm, actually 350 × ~110 chars ≈ 38 KB. Plus matrix tables 70 rows × ~90 chars ≈ 6.3 KB. Total maybe 50 KB. That's large but acceptable? The response could get very long. Alternatively, I can skip config_level and rely on matrix_tables + model_level for Q3 (which is the key model-defining flow).\n\nHmm. Let me think about what best serves the consumer. The matrix_tables fully preserve data. model_level for \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" per model would be ambiguous (two values per model). Better: make models = (size, variant, class)? That explodes models to 70.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: define models as DN sizes with attributes indicating variants; and config_level with the flow attributes. I'll go with config_level fully populated. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe put the data in config_level with conditions like \"DN40 | Reduced bore, battery/renewable powered | class 1 & 2\".\n\nActually, wait — maybe a cleaner mapping: model_level where model = \"DN40\" etc. for attributes that are common across variants? Check: is Q3 per size same across variants? DN40: reduced=25, full=40 → differ. So no.\n\nOK here's my decision: I'll provide:\n- matrix_tables (2 tables, all rows)\n- model_family.models: 28 DN sizes\n- characteristics.config_level: 5 attribute entries with axis \"meter_size × accuracy_class × power_supply_variant\" and 70 values each. Hmm, that's really long.\n\nAlternatively, reduce config_level to axis = \"variant\" with two entries per attribute (one per variant table), conditions being \"DN40, class 1 & 2\" etc. That still is 70 values per attribute total across two entries. Same volume.\n\nLet me just estimate more carefully and decide whether to include. Each values item: {\"condition\": \"DN40, accuracy class 1 & 2\", \"value\": 0.0625} ≈ 60 chars. 70 items × 5 attributes = 350 × 60 = 21 KB. Plus JSON overhead. Manageable-ish. Total response maybe 35–40 KB. That's long but I can do it. Hmm, but is it the best use? The rules emphasize matrix detection as MOST IMPORTANT and to populate both matrix_tables and model_level. Since model dimension here is DN size but values depend on config too, config_level is the schema-appropriate place (\"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model ...)\"). Here attributes vary by BOTH model (size) and config. The matrix_tables catch it all.\n\nCompromise: Include config_level but with combined condition strings, and skip model_level to avoid duplication. But rule 4 example populates model_level for 2-D tables. For 3+ dims, matrix_tables is \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — these fit matrix_tables precisely. I'll include both matrix_tables and config_level (fully), plus model_family.models.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider whether model_level should exist with model keys encoding size only and value possibly a list per config... No, values must be single.\n\nDecision: \n- matrix_tables: 2 (full fidelity)\n- model_family: family_name null, models: 28 sizes\n- characteristics.config_level: 5 entries (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1), axis: \"bore_type / power_source / meter_size / accuracy_class\", values: 70 items each.\n\nWait — that's 350 value entries. Let me reconsider trimming: Could I instead put config_level entries per attribute per variant (2 entries per attribute → 10 entries), each with conditions \"DNxx, class y\"? Total items same 350. Same.\n\nAlternatively skip config_level entirely and only give matrix_tables + model_family + type_level (accuracy classes as list?) — but then rule 4's spirit (populate model_level) is violated. Yet for multi-dim, matrix_tables is the designated container. I think including config_level adds machine-readable value; I'll include it. It's long but let's do it. Actually, you know, let me reconsider the balance: the response token limit is a real concern. 350 entries × ~15 tokens = 5250 tokens, plus tables ~2000 tokens, plus other ~500. Total ~8000 tokens. Should be fine within limits.\n\nLet me now also capture the Q-flow constraints text as... it's instructional text. Could go into matrix_tables description or certificate description. I'll add it as description in each matrix table (\"Flow rates Q1–Q4 can be freely chosen provided Q3 and Q3/Q1 from R49-1:2013 §4.1; Q1, Q2 minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3, Q4 maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 ≥ 40.\"). Good.\n\ntype_level characteristics: accuracy_classes 1 and 2 → could be \"accuracy_classes: {value: [1,2]}\"? Schema type_level values are single structured values. Lists as values... rule says lists of Emax values are model matrix. Accuracy class 1 & 2 applies to all. I could emit type_level: {\"accuracy_classes\": {\"value\": [1, 2], \"unit_symbol\": null}}? The value shape allows number | {min,max} | string — not arrays. Hmm. I'll skip type_level accuracy and rely on recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2].\n\nAlso power source variants: reduced bore = battery/renewable energy powered; full bore = AC mains. Could add config-ish type_level? It's part of the axis conditions. Fine.\n\nNow, models list: 28 entries: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650.\n\nCount: 28. ✓\n\nNow build config_level values. Condition format: \"Reduced bore, battery/renewable energy powered, DN65, accuracy class 1\". Include variant in each condition to be self-contained. Let me define short but explicit conditions:\n\nFor table 3 (reduced bore): condition = \"Reduced bore (battery/renewable energy powered); DN40; class 1 & 2\" — I'll use consistent format: \"DN40 | Reduced bore, battery/renewable powered | class 1 & 2\". Hmm, pick a readable one: \"Reduced bore, battery/renewable powered, DN40, class 1 & 2\".\n\nFor table 4: \"Full bore, AC mains powered, DN700, class 1\".\n\nAttributes:\n1. minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n2. transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n3. permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n4. overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n5. ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless → unit_symbol null)\n\nValues (structured per rule 2? For config_level the example shows plain value with unit at entry level; rule 5 says values follow same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. To be safe, put unit_symbol both at entry level and keep values as plain numbers... The R137 model_level example uses plain values with attribute-level unit_symbol. I'll follow that: entry-level unit_symbol, plain numeric values. Actually rule 5 says \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" — maybe value objects include unit_symbol per item. To satisfy both, I'll include unit_symbol at entry level only, matching the R137 example style. Hmm, risk either way; the example in rule 4 is authoritative for model_level; rule 5's phrasing \"same structured form\" likely refers to value separation from units. I'll add unit_symbol at the config_level entry level AND keep values plain numbers. Good enough.\n\nLet me now write all rows.\n\nReduced bore (26 rows):\n\nDN40 class 1&2: q1 0.0625, q2 0.1, q3 25, q4 31.25, ratio 400\nDN50 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\nDN65 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\nDN65 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\nDN80 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\nDN80 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 40